1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,736
The sunlight reaching your eyes right now
left the surface of the Sun about eight

2
00:00:05,736 --> 00:00:06,526
minutes ago.

3
00:00:06,806 --> 00:00:12,453
But the energy carried by that light began
its journey much earlier, deep in the

4
00:00:12,453 --> 00:00:18,312
core, and estimates for how long it took
to work its way out run from about 170,000

5
00:00:18,312 --> 00:00:20,147
years to around a million.

6
00:00:20,427 --> 00:00:22,435
So what is it travelling through?

7
00:00:22,715 --> 00:00:28,374
The Sun has no solid surface and no crust,
but it does have layers, each with its own

8
00:00:28,374 --> 00:00:28,773
rules.

9
00:00:29,053 --> 00:00:34,575
Let's go from the centre to the edge, one
layer at a time, and end with one of the

10
00:00:34,575 --> 00:00:40,300
strangest open questions in solar physics:
why the Sun's outer atmosphere is hundreds

11
00:00:40,300 --> 00:00:42,522
of times hotter than its surface.

12
00:00:43,422 --> 00:00:45,184
First, a sense of scale.

13
00:00:45,464 --> 00:00:51,693
The Sun is about 1.4 million kilometres
across, roughly a hundred times wider than

14
00:00:51,693 --> 00:00:52,149
Earth.

15
00:00:52,429 --> 00:00:55,970
You could fit about 1.3 million Earths
inside it.

16
00:00:56,250 --> 00:01:00,965
And it holds 99.8 percent of all the mass
in the solar system.

17
00:01:01,245 --> 00:01:05,758
Everything else, every planet, moon and
asteroid, is the leftovers.

18
00:01:06,038 --> 00:01:08,347
All of that mass is pulling inward.

19
00:01:08,627 --> 00:01:13,743
The only reason the Sun does not collapse
is that something in the middle is pushing

20
00:01:13,743 --> 00:01:14,048
back.

21
00:01:14,948 --> 00:01:19,327
Layer one is the core, the innermost
quarter of the Sun's radius.

22
00:01:19,607 --> 00:01:25,325
The temperature here is about 15 million
degrees Celsius, and the material is

23
00:01:25,325 --> 00:01:30,671
packed to around 150 grams per cubic
centimetre, roughly eight times the

24
00:01:30,671 --> 00:01:31,859
density of gold.

25
00:01:32,139 --> 00:01:37,636
Under those conditions, hydrogen nuclei
collide hard enough to fuse into helium,

26
00:01:37,636 --> 00:01:38,804
releasing energy.

27
00:01:39,084 --> 00:01:41,471
The burning is concentrated here.

28
00:01:41,751 --> 00:01:46,852
By the edge of the core, about 175,000
kilometres from the centre, the

29
00:01:46,852 --> 00:01:51,588
temperature has dropped to roughly half,
and fusion almost stops.

30
00:01:51,868 --> 00:01:55,471
That energy is the push that holds the
whole star up.

31
00:01:55,751 --> 00:01:59,991
Most of it comes from a chain of reactions
that starts with hydrogen.

32
00:02:00,271 --> 00:02:05,383
But a small share, about one percent,
comes from a different route that uses

33
00:02:05,383 --> 00:02:08,007
carbon, nitrogen and oxygen as helpers.

34
00:02:08,287 --> 00:02:11,405
For decades that second route was only
theory.

35
00:02:11,685 --> 00:02:18,114
Then, in 2020, the Borexino experiment,
buried under a mountain in Italy, detected

36
00:02:18,114 --> 00:02:19,525
neutrinos from it.

37
00:02:19,805 --> 00:02:24,979
Neutrinos barely interact with anything,
so they fly straight out of the core, and

38
00:02:24,979 --> 00:02:29,395
catching them is the closest thing we have
to looking directly inside.

39
00:02:29,675 --> 00:02:34,491
For years, detectors caught fewer solar
neutrinos than theory predicted.

40
00:02:34,771 --> 00:02:39,886
That puzzle was finally resolved when
physicists discovered that neutrinos have

41
00:02:39,886 --> 00:02:43,511
mass, which lets them change type on their
way to Earth.

42
00:02:44,411 --> 00:02:49,604
Layer two is the radiative zone, from
about a quarter of the way out to about

43
00:02:49,604 --> 00:02:51,627
seventy percent of the radius.

44
00:02:51,907 --> 00:02:55,591
Here, energy travels as light, but not in
a straight line.

45
00:02:55,871 --> 00:03:01,642
The plasma is so dense that each bit of
light is absorbed and re-emitted again and

46
00:03:01,642 --> 00:03:03,613
again, in random directions.

47
00:03:03,893 --> 00:03:08,989
It is like trying to leave a stadium crowd
where every person you bump into spins you

48
00:03:08,989 --> 00:03:09,409
around.

49
00:03:09,689 --> 00:03:15,513
Across this zone, the temperature falls
from about 7 million degrees to about 2

50
00:03:15,513 --> 00:03:21,779
million, and the density drops from around
20 grams per cubic centimetre to just 0.2,

51
00:03:21,779 --> 00:03:23,401
less dense than water.

52
00:03:23,681 --> 00:03:28,468
This is the slow part of the journey, and
the main reason the trip out takes so

53
00:03:28,468 --> 00:03:28,771
long.

54
00:03:29,051 --> 00:03:33,260
How long, exactly, depends on the model
and on what you count.

55
00:03:33,540 --> 00:03:40,501
NASA's Sun fact sheet gives about 170,000
years for energy to reach the top of the

56
00:03:40,501 --> 00:03:41,859
convection zone.

57
00:03:42,139 --> 00:03:47,840
NASA's Marshall solar physics group puts a
photon's trip across the radiative zone

58
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:49,995
alone at about a million years.

59
00:03:50,275 --> 00:03:55,641
Either way, the energy in today's sunlight
set off long before the first cities were

60
00:03:55,641 --> 00:03:56,024
built.

61
00:03:56,304 --> 00:04:01,243
At the top of this zone sits a thin
boundary called the tachocline, where

62
00:04:01,243 --> 00:04:04,965
scientists think the Sun's magnetic field
is generated.

63
00:04:05,865 --> 00:04:11,528
Layer three is the convection zone, the
outer thirty percent, starting about

64
00:04:11,528 --> 00:04:14,285
200,000 kilometres below the surface.

65
00:04:14,565 --> 00:04:19,617
Here the plasma is cool enough, below
about two million degrees, that light can

66
00:04:19,617 --> 00:04:22,431
no longer push the energy along
efficiently.

67
00:04:22,711 --> 00:04:24,589
So the Sun starts to boil.

68
00:04:24,869 --> 00:04:29,786
Hot plasma rises, cools at the top, and
sinks again, in enormous loops.

69
00:04:30,066 --> 00:04:35,445
You can see the tops of these loops on the
surface as a grainy pattern of granules.

70
00:04:35,725 --> 00:04:41,003
By the time the plasma reaches the top, it
is astonishingly thin, about ten thousand

71
00:04:41,003 --> 00:04:44,019
times less dense than the air you are
breathing.

72
00:04:44,299 --> 00:04:49,530
This churning motion also twists the
magnetic field, which is why the surface

73
00:04:49,530 --> 00:04:52,519
is covered in sunspots, loops and
eruptions.

74
00:04:53,419 --> 00:04:55,206
At last, the light escapes.

75
00:04:55,486 --> 00:05:00,396
The photosphere is what we call the Sun's
surface, although it is not solid.

76
00:05:00,676 --> 00:05:07,627
It is a layer about 400 kilometres thick,
at roughly 5,500 degrees Celsius.

77
00:05:07,907 --> 00:05:11,568
From here, the light needs only those
eight minutes to reach us.

78
00:05:11,848 --> 00:05:13,771
Above it, things get strange.

79
00:05:14,051 --> 00:05:19,595
In the next layer, the chromosphere, the
temperature climbs instead of falling,

80
00:05:19,595 --> 00:05:22,543
from about 6,000 to around 20,000 degrees.

81
00:05:22,823 --> 00:05:28,167
Then comes a thin transition region, and
above that the corona, the Sun's outer

82
00:05:28,167 --> 00:05:32,631
atmosphere, which reaches temperatures of
up to 2 million degrees.

83
00:05:32,911 --> 00:05:37,799
You normally cannot see the corona,
because the photosphere is far too bright.

84
00:05:38,079 --> 00:05:43,428
During a total solar eclipse, when the
Moon blocks the disc, it appears as a pale

85
00:05:43,428 --> 00:05:44,287
glowing halo.

86
00:05:45,187 --> 00:05:46,594
That is the puzzle.

87
00:05:46,874 --> 00:05:51,354
The further you move from the furnace at
the centre, the cooler things should get.

88
00:05:51,634 --> 00:05:56,563
Instead, the corona is hundreds of times
hotter than the surface beneath it.

89
00:05:56,843 --> 00:06:01,983
NASA describes the heating of the corona
as a major unsolved puzzle in

90
00:06:01,983 --> 00:06:02,938
heliophysics.

91
00:06:03,218 --> 00:06:07,180
To study it up close, NASA launched the
Parker Solar Probe.

92
00:06:07,460 --> 00:06:12,870
On the twenty-eighth of April 2021, it
crossed into the corona for the first

93
00:06:12,870 --> 00:06:18,778
time, passing a boundary called the Alfvén
critical surface, about 18.8 solar radii

94
00:06:18,778 --> 00:06:19,703
from the Sun.

95
00:06:19,983 --> 00:06:24,821
It was the first time a spacecraft had
flown through the Sun's atmosphere,

96
00:06:24,821 --> 00:06:29,593
measuring the particles and magnetic
fields where the solar wind is born.

97
00:06:29,873 --> 00:06:32,175
The answer is not settled yet.

98
00:06:32,455 --> 00:06:36,938
But for the first time, we are taking
measurements from inside the place where

99
00:06:36,938 --> 00:06:37,570
it happens.

100
00:06:38,470 --> 00:06:44,124
The Sun is about 4.6 billion years old and
a little less than halfway through its

101
00:06:44,124 --> 00:06:44,473
life.

102
00:06:44,753 --> 00:06:50,218
In about five billion years, it will swell
into a red giant, possibly engulfing

103
00:06:50,218 --> 00:06:55,130
Mercury, Venus, and perhaps Earth, before
shrinking into a white dwarf.

104
00:06:55,410 --> 00:07:00,697
Until then, the same layered engine keeps
running: fusion in the core, a long slow

105
00:07:00,697 --> 00:07:05,790
glow through the radiative zone, boiling
in the convection zone, and light that

106
00:07:05,790 --> 00:07:08,691
finally escapes, eight minutes away from
you.

107
00:07:08,971 --> 00:07:14,237
If you want more space explained with real
images and real numbers, subscribe.

108
00:07:14,517 --> 00:07:17,304
Every source is linked in the description.
